---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About the film"
  description="Golden Wings: Stewardess to Sky Queen follows Robyn Stewart and the aviation work her father Jay R. Ricks did on the Boeing 747 training program."
  path="/about-the-film"
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container prose">
      <span class="eyebrow">About the film</span>
      <h1>Story</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        <em>Golden Wings: Stewardess to Sky Queen</em> follows Robyn Stewart, an American Airlines
        flight attendant since 1971, and the aviation work her father Jay R. Ricks did on the Boeing
        747 training program. A short documentary about a mother, a grandfather, and an American
        Airlines family that stayed in the air.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-tight">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="eyebrow">Watch</span>
      <h2>Trailer and screener</h2>
      <p class="muted">
        Trailer on the Watch page. Runtime about 22 minutes. Screener on request for programmers.
      </p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/watch">Watch</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="keep-heading">
    <div class="container prose">
      <span class="eyebrow">Cabin life</span>
      <h2 id="keep-heading">Fifty years aloft</h2>
      <p>
        Robyn Stewart: flight attendant for more than fifty years, a living bridge between aviation's
        past and present. She began flying before the Boeing 747's first commercial flight and stayed
        aloft through seismic industry shifts: airline deregulation, the industry's glittering golden
        age and its long hangover, and the trauma of September 11th. She buried her husband Henry in
        Frankfurt, walked into rehab, walked back out, and returned to the job she loved.
      </p>
      <p>
        Her son Caleb grew up in airport terminals and hotel lobbies, raised by a mother whose office
        was at 35,000 feet. What began as a nine-minute college film project became something much
        larger.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-tight" aria-labelledby="people-heading">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="eyebrow">People</span>
      <h2 id="people-heading">Who the film keeps close</h2>
      <div class="card-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3><a href="/people/robyn-stewart">Robyn Stewart</a></h3>
          <p>Subject. Flight attendant since 1971.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3><a href="/people/jay-r-ricks">Jay R. Ricks</a></h3>
          <p>Papa. 747 training program.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3><a href="/people/jock-bethune">Jock Bethune</a></h3>
          <p>On camera. C.R. Smith Museum interview.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3><a href="/people/millie-alford">Millie Alford</a></h3>
          <p>Stewardess College research in development.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p class="muted" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
        <a href="/people">All people</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose">
      <span class="eyebrow">The work</span>
      <h2>What the film keeps</h2>
      <p>
        Honor flight attendants. Keep oral history and archive from the early jets through the
        modern cabin. Tell how training, safety work, labor, and the cabin itself changed.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where archive footage of the 747 cost too much, the film rebuilds the livery with
        <strong>synthetic media</strong>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
